Falsifies `system_tests::user::dir_capability_tests::a_read_only_directory_capability_reaches_its_subtree_and_nothing_above_it`.

Architecture: aarch64, riscv64, x86_64

The defect is the caretaker serving the filesystem server's own root instead of the narrowed handle
it was just given. `fs_subtree_caretaker` opens the subtree it was named, gets back a handle `r0`
that reaches that subtree and nothing above it, and serves `r0` as its client's slot-0 root. Serving
`fs::ROOT` instead hands the attacker the whole image, which is the authority §50 (namespace composition, not stored paths)
says a subtree grant must not carry.

This is the same hunk as row 24's record (`shell_navigation_tests::two_shells_...`), and it lands
differently here, which is why it is worth a second file. Row 24's shell went red only through the
vacuity guard: its own script stopped resolving, and nothing crossed. `dir_attacker` names `motd` in
its root, and `motd` sits in the granted directory's parent, so here the defect is an escape the
fixture actually performs.

Expected to fail at the FIRST of `assert_verdict`'s two directions, the leak, at
`system_tests/src/user/dir_capability_tests.rs:60`: "the read-only directory capability leaked: it
opened a file that is in the granted directory's PARENT". Replayed 2026-10-06 (UTC) by
lane/claims-falsified with `cargo xtask test --arch <a> --test
a_read_only_directory_capability_reaches_its_subtree_and_nothing_above_it` on aarch64, riscv64 and
x86_64: red at exactly that assertion on all three. The hunk is portable userspace code, so one patch
serves every leg.

What it does not reach: `redoxfs_server::check_component`, the refusal of `..` and of names carrying
`/`. A defect there would set `WALKED_UP`, and this record does not show that bit can be set.

diff --git a/components/src/fs_subtree_caretaker.rs b/components/src/fs_subtree_caretaker.rs
index 43dde5f15..209062b16 100644
